<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Voltaire</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1694">1694</a>–<a href="/years/1778">1778</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as François-Marie Arouet</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Voltaire, the pen name of François-Marie Arouet, was a French writer and philosopher and the most famous figure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>. In plays, histories, satires such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/candide">Candide</a> and polemics he attacked religious intolerance, the power of the Church and arbitrary government. He corresponded with rulers including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and spent his last decades near the Swiss border at Ferney.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/voltaire.jpg" alt="Voltaire" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nicolas de Largillière · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nicolas_de_Largilli%C3%A8re_-_Portrait_de_Voltaire_(1694-1778)_en_1718_-_P208_-_mus%C3%A9e_Carnavalet_-_5_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Enlightenment of Montesquieu, <b>Voltaire</b>, Rousseau and Diderot's Encyclopédie was largely conducted in French.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Frederick II, who corresponded with <b>Voltaire</b> and wrote on politics, history and war, became the model of the enlightened absolutist.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>John Gabriel Stedman's illustrated Narrative of a Five Years Expedition against the Revolted Negroes of Surinam, published in London in 1796 with engravings by William Blake among others, shaped European debates on slavery, and <b>Voltaire</b> set the scene of a mutilated slave in Candide of 1759 near Suriname.</blockquote><a href="/empire/suriname">Suriname · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Confucian ethics, centred on humaneness, reciprocity and the duties of relationships, offered an alternative to ethics founded on divine command, and the Jesuit missionaries who translated the Analects into Latin in the 17th century made Confucius admired by thinkers of the European Enlightenment such as Leibniz and <b>Voltaire</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/confucianism">Confucianism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Locke’s Letter Concerning Toleration of 1689 argued that the state had no business forcing belief, and <b>Voltaire</b>’s Treatise on Tolerance of 1763 was written after the Protestant merchant Jean Calas was executed in Toulouse in 1762 on a false charge of murdering his son to stop him becoming…</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Voltaire</b> popularised it in France, Émilie du Châtelet translated the Principia into French, and the confidence that reason and experiment could uncover the laws of nature, and perhaps of society, passed into the 18th century.</blockquote><a href="/movement/scientific-revolution">Scientific Revolution · Outcomes</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1748" aria-label="Paris on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4604 458 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Voltaire"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5054 728h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1748">What was here in 1748? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;There the Jew, the Mahometan, and the Christian transact together, as though they all professed the same religion, and give the name of infidel to none but bankrupts.&#8221;</p><footer>Letters on the English, letter 6 &#183; English translation of 1733 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2445/pg2445-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;If one religion only were allowed in England, the Government would very possibly become arbitrary; if there were but two, the people would cut one another’s throats; but as there are such a multitude, they all live happy and in peace.&#8221;</p><footer>Letters on the English, letter 6 &#183; English translation of 1733 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2445/pg2445-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I and my children cultivate them; our labour preserves us from three great evils—weariness, vice, and want.&#8221;</p><footer>Candide, chapter 30 &#183; anonymous translation (Boni and Liveright, 1918) &#183; spoken by an old Turk in the tale &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/19942/pg19942-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;There is no doubt of it; but in this country it is found good, from time to time, to kill one Admiral to encourage the others.&#8221;</p><footer>Candide, chapter 23 &#183; anonymous translation (Boni and Liveright, 1918) &#183; an Englishman at Portsmouth, on the shooting of Admiral Byng &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/19942/pg19942-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;If this is the best of possible worlds, what then are the others?&#8221;</p><footer>Candide, chapter 6 &#183; anonymous translation (Boni and Liveright, 1918) &#183; spoken by Candide &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/19942/pg19942-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;He proved admirably that there is no effect without a cause, and that, in this best of all possible worlds, the Baron's castle was the most magnificent of castles, and his lady the best of all possible Baronesses.&#8221;</p><footer>Candide, chapter 1 &#183; anonymous translation (Boni and Liveright, 1918) &#183; of the philosopher Pangloss &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/19942/pg19942-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
